<chapter>
  <chaptertitle>Aspects <ampersand/> Afflictions<label>ch:aspects</label></chaptertitle>
  <slug>Some rise by sin, and some by virtue fall.</slug>

  <p>
    Malleus Deum uses Aspects as a game mechanic to model inner conflict for
    PCs. They represent the conflict between man and himself.
  </p>


  <section>
    <sectiontitle>Aspects are Abilities</sectiontitle>
    
    <p>      
      Aspects are antagonistic abilities attached to a PC that can describe a
      range of different features the character has that are not covered by
      other character attributes. The PCs may be compelled by the GM to save
      against one of their aspects, at any time that context dictates that the
      PCs actions might be directed by that aspect.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      FIXME: aspects are characterized by a phrase... 
      Each aspect has a rank that represents how much control the PC has over
      that particular aspect.  The higher their rank the easier it is for the
      character to resist the aspect. Players have to keep track of the rank,
      and possibly the stage of any aspects they have on their own character
      sheet.
    </p>

    <playexample>
      <p>
	Dourli has an aspect <emph>Terrible Braggart</emph>.  He's playing cards
	at the local inn, trying to gather some information from the regulars,
	when the topic of a recent jewel heist from a local merchant is raised.
	The GM uses Douli's aspect to try to compel him to claim responsibility
	for the heist (whether he is responsible or not). Dourli must make an
	Aspect Save or be compelled to be Terrible Braggert.
      </p>
      
      <p>
	If Dourli fails the save then he is compelled. <emph>It is up to the
	player just how his PC complies with the aspect</emph>, he might
	disparage the job, saying that <quotemarks>it sounds like child's play,
	a three legged scrofulous dog could have pulled it off. I'd done harder
	jobs before I grew a beard</quotemarks>, or he might take credit for it
	and say, <quotemarks>it's funny you should bring that up because you are
	looking at the dwarf responsible for said heist, and, if I do say so
	myself, only a plunderer of the highest calibre could have pulled it off
	so professionally.</quotemarks>.  Or the player could frame someone
	else <quotemarks>It was old Jon Longshanks that did it.  He's the only
	one round here that's got the know-how to do a job like this, and it's
	got his mark on it if I'm not mistaken, and I never am.  I should know
	there ain't a thing about lifting that I didn't teach him</quotemarks>.
      </p>
    </playexample>
    

